A||||40||batboy||oh good grief, clutch problems||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||01-27-2000||07:05 PM||||For 3 years I drove an automatic Fiero and life was good. I heard many other Fiero owners cussing their clutches. Then I bought a four speed 85 GT, sure those clutch stories were scary, but shucks, it'll never happen to me. Five months pass by and my clutch works just fine. BUT, last night I turned off the ol' Turnpike and pushed in the clutch as I rolled up to the toll both. The clutch petal felt funny like it had a lot of play in it and released very low to the floor when I pulled away and shifted through the gears. This morning I pushed in the clutch and engaged the starter. The car began lurking forward like I didn't have the clutch pushed in (but of course I did). I shifted to neutral and started it, but even with the clutch pushed in as far as I could push it, it would not go into gear without grinding. Ain't it the pits, it's cold and it's snowing and my primary driver just died. The hydraulics part of the clutch seem ok, does this mean this clutch disk fried? It never once slipped when I was driving it home last night. I'd appreciate any ideas of what to look for first from you veterans of the Fiero Clutch Wars. It does have an aluminum pedal that is slightly bent, but it's no worse now then when I bent it racing a CRX last summer. I actually have a new steel one in the trunk, just never got around to installing it. Pretty sure it's not the pedal though. Besides, it was working ok... I drove at highway speeds for a couple hours... then it was messed up??? Weird!||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||01-27-2000||07:19 PM||||A fried disk SHOULD give the opposite problem (slipping). It really sounds like the hydraulics. Especially since it seems to have gotten worse just sitting overnight. I guess you've read at least some of the 24,000 post on THAT subject.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh... just thought of another maybe... seems like someone on here had a problem with the (whatever you call that thing that pivots between the slave cyl and release bearing) breaking.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-27-2000||07:21 PM||||Batty,<P>I can't add too much here... but although as unlikely as it sounds... it sure seems like it would be the pedal.  Check your slave travel.  That should tell you.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sage||01-27-2000||07:22 PM||||If the fluid is full and it doesn't "pump up" at all, I would check the slave cyl.mounting point and make sure it hasn't come loose or broken, then the pivot arm on the tranny shaft, tho it is hard to imagine this becoming loose, other than that, it sounds like you lost the preasure plate.  I have seen them go out with no warning signs what-so-ever.  Could be way off base here, but thought I would put my .002 in.  Maybe one of the more experienced fieroholics has some other suggestions before you start tearing stuff apart.  Keep in mind that more than one mech. has demonstrated that the clutch assembly can be accessed and changed without removing the cradle or even the tranny.  Supposedly can be done in less than 5 hrs.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 01-27-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000004||RICK||01-27-2000||07:37 PM||||Batboy..I had the same exact problem in my first 85GT.  I was going down the highway for about 2 hours, got off on an exit ramp, pushed the clutch in to stop and nothing, it went right to the floor.  The problem was the slave cyl.<BR>Good luck||||reg||9
Z||000005||Mark||01-27-2000||08:27 PM||||It seems I've heard of alot of problems with either broken slave cylinder mounting brackets and also broken clutch forks (or whatever the lever is called that applies pressure to the throwout bearing).  Check for movement in the slave.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||01-27-2000||08:28 PM||||So you weren't the only one outside in the snow,fixing their clutch;I had the same symptoms just a couple nights ago.My resevoir lid was on so tight that I had to remove the resevoir,take it indoors out of the 12DEG weather,by the way,nothing spilled out when I took the resevoir out,but after adding fluid,all is well without even bleeding the thing(cylinder had fluid,just not the resevoir).<BR>Lucky I suppose,for now anyways.<BR>I'd think that the change in engagement point would indicate that it was a linkage/actuation problem rather than anything with the disc.Difficulty engaging gears after a disc has been warped,or has pieces of itself broken off and taking up space/clearance between the pressure plate and flywheel is usually different than you described.<BR>Does anyone recall the proper amount of travel at the clutch fork?<BR>I'd be checking there first.||||reg||1
Z||000007||squid||01-27-2000||09:34 PM||||If you lost fluid, its the slave. If you didnt, its the master. I have the aluminum pedal on my '85 v6 SE and the only problem I ever had with it was the part you step on came loose from the arm so I TIG welded it in '94. No problem. My daily driver is the one I told you I'm putting the 4.3 in to awhile back'87 2.5/auto. I hate to take apart something that is working great.Maybe something serious will break soon. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||01-27-2000||09:58 PM||||<BR><B>batboy</B>, I had the same problem with my clutch. Shortly after I had done my ATF transfusion (which continues to work fine), I had one hiccup. I was driving along, the clutch was working fine one shift, the clutch was NOT working fine the next. It turned out that the slave had simply inhaled a quantity of air between shifts. There was no slow deterioration of the shifting. It happened in an instant. I bled the clutch one more time and have not had a problem since. That was about five months ago.||||reg||4
Z||000009||batboy||01-27-2000||10:25 PM||||I started going outside to check the clutch fluid level, but it's really snowing heavy here now. Screw it! The Suburban 4X4 still works ok, so I ain't messing with the Fiero tonight. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Harry||01-27-2000||10:52 PM||||Patrick, just how does a person change the clutch without pulling the tranny or cradle?<BR>Theres no room.||||reg||4
Z||000011||Patrick||01-28-2000||12:12 AM||||<BR><B>Harry</B>, I couldn't tell ya. I've never had to get that far into my clutch. My problems have always involved just the hydraulics. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||David Gamache||01-28-2000||12:24 AM||||Harry, the clutch can only be replaced be supporting the motor with a top side brace and dropping the cradle to slip off the trans. This leaves the motor in the car, but everything else still has to be removed. Without an upper brace the whole engine and trans and cradle would have to be removed.||||reg||1
Z||000013||AL||01-28-2000||12:38 AM||||thank god i have an automatic!||||reg||14
Z||000014||Patrick||01-28-2000||02:11 AM||||<BR>Yeah, well we have a devil of a good time driving our stick-shifts! (when our clutches are working properly <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||6
Z||000015||WKDFIRO||01-28-2000||02:12 AM||||Don't say that AL!<P>Just wait until you find out the joy and delight that is changing out the torque converter solenoid in your tranny!<P>As for taking out a clutch without taking off touching the cradle OR the tranny? Who ever told you that better lay off the wacky tabbacy! Either that or I and a lot of mechanics are going to feel REAL dumb...<P>With 3 of us triple teaming the car we got the car in the air and the tranny on the ground in a little more than 2 hours. WooHoo!<P>Batboy, I think that the general consensus is that you have a mere hydraulic problem with just one member who would rather mock your manual than sympathize. <P>I won't mention any names but his first letter is A and the last letter is L...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-28-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000016||BOILERMAKER||01-28-2000||07:29 AM||||I've had my 5spd Getrag for over 4 years now and yes I did replace my clutch, but it was because I already had the engine out.  No problems at all here.<P>Maybe you already checked this, but the 85 does have the aluminum pedal and you may want to give that a look unless you already replaced it.||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||01-28-2000||07:34 AM||||Thanks guys, I feel a little better. I hope it is just the hydraulics, even if it can be a hassle. I've fried many clutches in my time (before my Fiero days), but they always slipped first before going completely out, but I've never had to work on hydraulic clutch systems before. The ol' Chevelle clutch linkage had to be welded up a couple times, and the disk and pressure plate replace once. Al's cool! I know he's sort of joking. In fact I sort of said the something like that at the beginning of my first post. I have 2 auto Fieros and 1 manual. If I keep having clutch problems, I might just have 2 auto Fieros... LOL.||||reg||1
Z||000018||DRH||01-28-2000||01:42 PM||||batboy,<P>If the fluid is low and you don't see any indication of where it is leaking... Pull back the carpet and see if the padding is soaked. If the seal on the master cylinder goes out it leaks fluid inside the car (took me a couple of refills to figure that one out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||01-28-2000||06:36 PM||||Here is what little I have to update. The clutch fluid reservoir is full. The clutch does not work even if I pump it up. I can find nothing around the slave or the master that indicates any leaks. Next, I need an assistant to push the clutch pedal in and out while I watch to see what happens at the slave and tranny end. Maybe later this weekend. I hate winter! Damn snow! Stupid clutch!||||reg||1
Z||000020||Patrick||01-28-2000||08:24 PM||||<BR><B>batboy</B>, the clutch hydraulic system can draw air into itself <B>without</B> showing any signs of fluid leakage. Just so you know.||||reg||3
Z||000021||lowCG||01-28-2000||08:29 PM||||I agree wholeheartedly,seems that even though I got lucky with my clutch-fix the other night,I'm not immune from the cold.<BR>After sitting in the 0 degree weather,my car's suspension won't give much,tires neither,and I can feel the whole car flexing to make up for it,usually only for a couple blocks.This morning I pulled out onto the street over some frozen chunks of ice and my(ex-perfect)windshield went"SNICK".A crack formed by the lower corner.Just after describing how this had happened to another car from rock-hard springs in another post even.Guess I need some chassis work,a cage maybe,or just some well placed reinforcements.||||reg||1
Z||000022||fiero56||01-28-2000||09:10 PM||||Batboy, In theory you could use a broom stick to push the clutch pedal down while standing beside the slave to watch for movement. Unfortunatly, I haven't ever been able to find a stick long enough.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Sage||01-28-2000||09:50 PM||||Bryan;  It really sounds like you have a hydraulic problem or the pedal and/or linkage.  If it does turn out to be clutch failure,  do a search on changing clutch.  There was a thread on this forum within the last 2 mos. that explained in detail how to change the clutch without pulling the tranny, the engine, or even the cradle.  It had a link in it and even showed pictures.  Personally, I have found it easier, and not much more time consuming to just unhook the main harness at both ends, unhook the rear brake lines, take the struts out with the whole mess and pull the whole assembly. This link did show pics of the clearance you have by pulling the axles, removing the obvious mounts and several other items and just pulling the tranny over to the drivers side far enough to gain access to the clutch assembly.  I also know a mechanic here locally that told me he has done this in the fieros, before I ever saw the post with the link on this forum.  Seems like more work to me, cause of the limited space to work in the fiero.  I have come to the conclusion that <B>ANYHTING</B> major that needs done to the engine and/or tranny is best accomplished by pulling the whole mess out!  I did my sons 88 clutch by myself in about 8hrs.  Just had him come out and help bleed the brakes when I was done.  Not bragging, cause that is not that great a time anyway, just pointing out that it's not that big a deal. Oh yea, I was working inside.  Not out in the yard or drive...in the snow...in the cold.  I wouldn't do it there either!<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 01-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||AL||01-29-2000||12:02 AM||||sorry bat i wasn't mockng you, i was just appreciating the fact that i don't have to do that to my car, but i hope you get it running again. now as for you wkd fiero, i've been turning wrenches for 13 yrs i have done more work then what a fiero can dish out, so if my car does what you say i don't worry about it,cause when you work for a shop or dealer you can fixs things alot quicker then trying to do it at home,so once again, sorry bat but i wish you the best,AL.||||reg||1
Z||000025||AL||01-29-2000||12:02 AM||||sorry bat i wasn't mockng you, i was just appreciating the fact that i don't have to do that to my car, but i hope you get it running again. now as for you wkd fiero, i've been turning wrenches for 13 yrs i have done more work then what a fiero can dish out, so if my car does what you say i don't worry about it,cause when you work for a shop or dealer you can fixs things alot quicker then trying to do it at home,so once again, sorry bat but i wish you the best,AL.||||reg||10
Z||000027||WKDFIRO||01-29-2000||03:36 AM||||Perhaps mocking was too strong a word for what I meant as light hearted ribbing. If the poke was too sharp than I apologize whole heartedly. <P>I too am fortunate to have a shop to go to to take care of car business. But I would much rather be working on someone else's car and be paid for it than work on my own car for nothin!||||reg||11
