Z||000029||batboy||01-29-2000||09:22 AM||||***grin***  That little plain looking Batmobile surprises a lot of folks, but the 14 inch wheels are going bye bye this spring. I'm going to upgrade to a good used set of 15 or 16 inch wheels, whichever I can get the best bargain on. Then, the next step is BF Goodrich Comp T/A tires. Still a bit of a sleeper if you aren't paying attention and what non-Fiero guy would notice anyway, right?||||reg||1
Z||000030||Wolfhound||01-29-2000||10:11 AM||||Batboy, You should put a 2M4 Decal on the Batmobile. That would be a sleeper. ||||reg||7
Z||000031||MrFeeArrow||01-29-2000||10:37 AM||||I just had the clutch replaced on my 2M4 at Mr.Transmission here in Florida. They replaced the clutch without dropping the cradle all the way down. They say it's just as easy to remove the 2 aft cradle bolts and pivot the assembly down, then they removed the left axle (engine was supported from the floor with what looked like a strut). The whole process took about 4 hours. Looked easy enough that I may do it the next time.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Sage||01-29-2000||02:55 PM||||Just an after thought on your situation; you could try "backbleeding" the slave cyl. It was covered here in the not too distant past.  You push the rod inside the slave in (toward drivers side) and while holding it in, open the bleeder and then before releasing, close it up again.  Like I said, just an afterthought. Good luck, hope you get it figured out soon.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000033||AL||01-29-2000||11:00 PM||||don't get me wrong i too like to get paid but when you have friends and cool bosses that let you have the run of the place(after hour joys)you have a good time working on your car.now it's to bad your to far bat cause if i was nearby, we would have your baby back together,they don'tcall me, a bumper to bumper tech for nothin.good luck and we are all pullin for you bro, AL.||||reg||1
Z||000034||batboy||02-06-2000||07:13 PM||||On the road again! Happy, happy, joy, joy! Here's an update on my clutch problem. I'd been putting off working on it, waiting for nice weather and we finally warmed up nicely this weekend. The problem turned out to be a combination of sloppy linkage and a bent aluminum clutch pedal. The hole in the master cylinder linkage rod that attaches to the pedal pivot was worn and egg shaped. Also, since I had a new steel clutch pedal that I never put on, I used that to compare with the old aluminum one and discovered that the old pedal was bent worse than I thought. I replaced the pedal and everything is working again. At least it's driveable again.<P>That M/C rod hole is still kind of sloppy though. Does anyone know if I can remove and replace that activator rod without disconnecting the hydraulic line from the M/C or do you have to replace the whole M/C? I'd like to avoid bleeding the hydraulic system after hearing all those horror stories that others have had. Especially since the hydraulic part of the clutch has been working fine. Thanks to all who offered suggestions. It really helped me pinpoint the problem quicker. I feel better and so does my car.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Rodney||02-06-2000||08:36 PM||||If you replace an aluminum clutch pedal with the new design steel clutch pedal the pin is much smaller in diameter. So the banjo on the new smaller pin is very sloppy. Plus the old style is steel on steel which wears the hole in the banjo (The pin also). If the banjo is brand new it is a very precise fit. If the banjo is worn it is not as precise but still much better than not having it. I have these special machined oilite bushings for this. Not on my web page yet. $5.00 and $1.50 shipping. Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000036||2M4 Dale||02-06-2000||08:45 PM||||OK...I've just finished replacing the clutch master cylinder the slave cylinder and a complete new hydraulic line (from the Fiero store). According to everything I've read and been told the rod ought to push the lever on the trans 1" to 1 1/8". I've bled the system and I'm still only getting 7/8" to 15/16" of travel. My car has the steel clutch pedal allready. So I don't think thats the problem. Do I not have the system bled good enough ??? HELP !!!||||reg||9
Z||000037||Sage||02-06-2000||09:33 PM||||Glad to hear you're back in operation! Sounds like RD can fix you up on the "slop" problem. Enjoy!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000038||batboy||02-06-2000||10:00 PM||||Thanks Rodney! The dealer did say the book stated that I'd need a new M/C to match the new steel pedal, but they couldn't tell me why. I'd heard several others Forum members mention that they replaced their pedals, but there was never any word about replacing the M/C at the same time, so I just figured it was nothing real important. I guess it's time to call up Rodney and place an order. First I'd better visit his site again and decide if I need anything else too.||||reg||1
Z||000039||2M4 Dale||02-06-2000||11:05 PM||||Go to <A HREF="http://www.wrenchhead.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wrenchhead.com/</A>    I got my new master cylinder there for $67.00 including shipping.<p>[This message has been edited by 2M4 Dale (edited 02-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||batboy||02-06-2000||11:36 PM||||Thanks for the tip Dale, I have that URL bookmarked. Nothing wrong with my M/C except the hole in the rod or banjo or whatever it's called. I think I'd rather pay Rodney $5 plus shipping instead of buying a whole new one, which would mean having to bleed the whole dang system. I need to have this car back in operation as a daily driver immediately. Saving about $60 is good too.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Phil||02-07-2000||07:49 AM||||Bat <BR>I had the same situation with the banjo as you (oval hole). I went to the hardwear store and got an electric motor bearing and soldered it into position. The bushing so far works great.||||reg||1
